CHAPTER 1
July 1955
The Pruitt house always smelled like something good was about to come out of the oven, and on the first Saturday of July, it was cinnamon bread. Eleven-year-old Kat sat on the porch swing with her knees pulled up to her chest, waiting for her friend to stroll across the street.
"You're late," Kat said, not unkindly. It was just a fact, the way she said most things.
"My mother made me finish the ironing first." Elizabeth Garner — nobody but her mother called her Elizabeth — dropped her bike in the grass and climbed the porch steps two at a time. "Liz" suited her better anyway; there was nothing about the way she threw herself into the porch swing beside Kat, setting it rocking hard enough that they both had to grab the chain.
They had been best friends since they were seated next to each other on the first day of third grade — an arbitrary stroke of Mrs. Yoder's seating chart, Garner and Pruitt landing side by side for no reason either girl was ever able to reconstruct — and had been inseparable ever since, an accident of a classroom roster that neither girl had ever bothered to question.
Liz dug a folded magazine page out of her skirt pocket, soft at the creases, and smoothed it flat across her knees. It was a photograph torn from one of her older sister's magazines — a tall iron tower against a pale sky, a wide gray river, tiny dark figures strolling along a bridge below.
"My sister says this is the most beautiful place in the whole world," Liz said, tracing a finger along the tower's shape. "Paris, France. She wants to go someday, but she says she probably never will, because Mother says that's not the kind of thing girls from Ephrata do."
Kat leaned over to look, and something about the picture — the impossible height of the tower, the sheer, foreign distance of it — settled into her chest like a held breath. "We should go," she said. "You and me. When we're grown."
Liz looked up at her, delighted and a little stunned, as if the idea hadn't occurred to her until Kat said it out loud, and then, once said, had become instantly, obviously true. "Forever-forever?" she asked, borrowing the words before either of them quite knew yet that they'd become the shape of every promise the two of them ever made to each other.
"Forever-forever," Kat said.
Liz folded the picture carefully back along its creases and tucked it into her pocket, patting it once through the fabric as if sealing the deal. "I brought something else too," she said, reaching in again. She produced a folded square of paper with the solemnity of a magician revealing a rabbit. "I read about it in one of my sister's books. It's how you make somebody your sister forever. A blood oath."
Kat's eyes went wide. "Forever-forever?"
"Forever-forever," Liz confirmed. "Come on. My room, before my mother sees."
It would be years before either girl could explain why that particular afternoon had mattered so much — why, of all the ordinary days of that ordinary summer, this was the one that stuck. But some friendships announce themselves early, and quietly insist on being taken seriously.
Upstairs, Kat sat cross-legged on the bedroom floor, her skirt tucked neatly around her knees. In front of her, resting on a paper napkin, sat a glass tumbler of slowly melting ice, two green pastel Band-Aids, a box of matches, two sheets of pale pink stationery, and a single black fountain pen.
Feet hurried down the hallway, and Liz slipped back into her bedroom, closing the door quietly behind her. She dropped to the floor across from Kat.
"I found a safety pin in my mother's sewing kit," Liz whispered, holding up the little silver loop.
Between them lay the tools of their sacred business.
Liz struck a match against the rough side of the box and held the steel point of the pin in the flame until it blackened with soot.
"Careful, Liz, you're going to burn your fingers!" Kat warned, leaning back.
Liz blew out the match and set the spent stick on the napkin. She took a deep breath. "You ready, Kat?"
Kat closed her eyes. "Ready."
Together, the girls pressed their left thumbs against the melting ice cube inside the glass, counting silently to ten.
Kat pulled her thumb out first, wiping her wet palm on her skirt, and held out her pale, glistening hand. Liz didn't hesitate; she took Kat's hand and gave her thumb a swift, deliberate poke.
"Ow! Jeepers, Liz!" Kat gasped, flinching so hard her elbow knocked the glass over, sending ice cubes and water pooling across the floorboards. She stared at her thumb in utter betrayal as a bright, ruby bead of blood welled up on her skin. "You said the ice would make it so I couldn't feel it!"
"Shh! Do you want my mother to come up here?" Liz hissed, though her own heart was hammering against her ribs.
Quickly, Liz pricked her own thumb — wincing at the sharp nip — and before either bead could drip, she pressed her thumb firmly against Kat's. The wet smudge of blood sealed between them.
"One, two, three," Liz counted softly, her eyes locked on Kat's.
Together, their voices murmured in unison:
"Side by side, or miles apart, we are sisters at the heart. Secrets kept and promises made, a friendship that will never fade."
"Swear on your life," Liz breathed, her voice dropping into a solemn, sacred register. "Swear on your soul you'll take it to the grave."
"I swear," Kat whispered back, her temporary outrage completely melting into awe. "Cross my heart and hope to die, stick a needle in my eye."
They held their thumbs pressed tightly together for a long, quiet ten seconds before slowly pulling apart.
With the blood seal official, Liz uncapped her father's heavy Parker fountain pen. On the pink stationery, in her best, neatest cursive, she recorded the terms of their pact. She wrote it out twice so they would each have a copy. Beneath their signatures on both sheets, each girl pressed her pricked thumb onto the cotton paper, leaving two smudged, reddish-pink seals side by side.
